  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nWeak high pressure prevailing across the area this afternoon \ninto tonight will keep clear skies over the WFO MPX coverage \narea through this evening. However, the clouds noted on visible \nsatellite imagery over the Dakotas and southern Canada will \nslide across MN/WI from early morning Monday through most of the\nday Monday, due to the passage of a dry frontal system through \nthe Upper Midwest tomorrow. No appreciable change in airmass \nwill come with this frontal system on Monday, thus highs in the \n50s today will nudge upwards to the mid 50s to lower 60s on \nMonday. In the wake of this Monday frontal system, high \ntemperatures will be bumped back down to the 50s on Tuesday.\n\nLate Tuesday into Wednesday presents us with our only chance for \nrain throughout this forecast period. An area of low pressure \ndeveloping over WY Tuesday will shift eastward through the \nPlains Tuesday night and into the mid-Mississippi River Valley \nby Wednesday morning. High pressure to the north plus generally \nzonal flow aloft will keep the low to the south of the Upper \nMidwest, but enough lift to the north of the system plus height \nfalls with meager moisture advection may be able to squeeze out \na few hundredths of an inch of precip here and there. Overall, \nchances for precipitation are quite low so have kept PoPs \ngenerally no higher than Slight Chance range.\n\nBy late day Wednesday, Canadian-origin high pressure will slide \nsouth towards the international border, moving into the Northern\nPlains on Thursday and settling atop MN/WI Friday-Sunday. This \nwill result in clearing skies, appreciable cold air advection, \nand continued dry conditions across the region. Highs will drop \nto the 40s on Wednesday then to just the 30s on Thursday and \nFriday, with lows into the mid-20s to lower 30s area-wide in the\nearly mornings Friday and Saturday. Temperatures will start a \nslow rebound over the weekend into next week, but longer range \nmodels continue to show no appreciable systems for our area \nthrough at least the next 7-10 days.",
